   175	## 2. Torus-type decision — Oka/Tokunaga conic criterion
   176	
   177	**Definition.** `C` is of *(2,3)-torus type* if `F̄_j = G_2³ + G_3²` for homogeneous
   178	`G_i` of degree `i`. Scaling is free: replacing `(G_2,G_3)` by `(λG_2, μG_3)` with
   179	`λ³=μ²=κ` turns the identity into `κF̄_j`, so "up to `C^*`" costs nothing. Over `C`,
   180	`G_2³ + G_3² = G_2³ − (iG_3)²`, so the `±` convention costs nothing either.
   181	
   182	Two preliminary facts, both `PROVED-HERE`.
   183	
   184	**Lemma 2.1 (coprimality).** `G_2 ≠ 0`, `G_3 ≠ 0`, and `gcd(G_2,G_3)=1`.
   185	*Proof.* `G_2=0` gives `F̄=G_3²`, `G_3=0` gives `F̄=G_2³`, both non-reduced. If `h`
   186	is a common factor then `h³ | G_2³` and `h² | G_3²`, so `h² | F̄` — non-reduced. `[]`
   187	
   188	**Lemma 2.2 (no node is inner).** If `G_2(P)=G_3(P)=0` then `P` is *not* an ordinary
   189	node of `C`. *Proof.* Locally `f = a³+b²` with `a(P)=b(P)=0`. Then `ord_P f ≥ min(3,2)=2`,
   190	and the degree-2 part of `f` is the degree-2 part of `b²`, i.e. `b_1²` where `b_1` is the
   191	linear part of `b`. If `b_1 ≠ 0` the tangent cone is a *double line* (rank ≤ 1), not two
   192	distinct lines. If `b_1 = 0` then `ord_P b ≥ 2` and `ord_P f ≥ 3`. Either way `P` is not an
   193	`A_1`. `[]`
   194	
   195	Lemma 2.2 already **corrects the framing in the charge**: the charge budgets "passing
   196	through 3 nodes = 3 conditions". For this curve the conic must *avoid* the nodes; they
   197	cannot lie on it at all.
   198	
   199	### 2.1 First proof — the conic space is one-dimensional and its member is excluded
   200	
   201	Tokunaga's criterion, verbatim from Oka05 p. 3 (fetched, hashed above):
   202	
   203	> **Lemma 3.** *(Tokunaga Criterion [14])* The sextic `C` is of torus type if and only
   204	> if there exists a conic `C_2 : g_2(x,y)=0` such that `C_2 ∩ C ⊂ Σ(C)` and
   205	> `I(C,C_2;P) = 2ρ(P,5)` for any `P ∈ C ∩ C_2`.
   206	
   207	Only the **"only if"** direction is used, and in the concrete form supplied by the
   208	identity itself: for a torus sextic, `C_2 := V(G_2)` satisfies
   209	`C ∩ C_2 = V(G_2) ∩ V(G_3)` (if `G_2(P)=0` and `F̄(P)=0` then `G_3(P)²=0`) and
   210	`I(C,C_2;P) = I(G_3², G_2; P) = 2·I(G_2,G_3;P)`, whence `Σ_P I(C,C_2;P) = 12` by Bézout.
   211	
   212	By Lemma 2.2, `C ∩ C_2 ⊆ Σ(C) ∖ {3 nodes} = {Q_∞}`. So a torus structure forces a conic
   213	with
   214	
   215	```text
   216	   I(C, C_2; Q_∞) = 12 .                                                       (2.1)
   217	```
   218	
   219	Now compute the space of such conics **exactly**, using `(1.2)`/`(1.3)`. The orders of
   220	the six basis conics on the branch are `0,2,4,6,8,12`; since they are pairwise distinct,

