   285	## 3. Torus-type decision — the non-tame `A³ − B²` loophole
   286	
   287	### 3.1 Scope finding: the charged cancellation kill does **not** cover this identity
   288	
   289	The charge asks whether the charged `triple-cover-close` cancellation kill "covers
   290	exactly the `A³−B² = c·F` identity needed here, all scaling conventions matched".
   291	**It does not, and the mismatch is directional, not a matter of conventions.**
   292	
   293	*Conventions do match.* Dehomogenising a torus identity at `Z=1` gives
   294	`F = A³ + B²` with `A = G_2|_{Z=1}` of degree `≤ 2` and `B = G_3|_{Z=1}` of degree `≤ 3`;
   295	replacing `B` by `iB` gives `A³ − B² = F`. Conversely, homogenising `A³ − B² = F` to
   296	degree `6` returns `(A^h)³ − (B^h)² = F̄` because `deg F = 6` exactly. So
   297	
   298	```text
   299	   C is of torus type  ⟺  ∃ A,B ∈ C[x,y] :  A³ − B² = F ,  deg A ≤ 2 , deg B ≤ 3 .   (3.1)
   300	```
   301	
   302	*But the charged lemma runs the other way.* Charged `triple-cover-close` §4 opens the
   303	numerical bands `(deg A, deg B) = (2k,3k)`, `k ≥ 2`, i.e. `(4,6),(6,9),(8,12),(10,15)`
   304	(display (4.4)), and §5 eliminates all four, concluding display (5.6):
   305	
   306	```text
   307	   deg A ≤ 2   and   deg B ≤ 3 .
   308	```
   309	
   310	That is the **conclusion** `(3.1)` needs as a **hypothesis**. The charged lemma
   311	therefore *delivers the torus band*; it does not exclude it. The kill of the low band in
   312	charged §6 is outsourced to the *uncharged* `triple-cover-r2` report (its "leading-form
   313	classification, lines 167–222" and "infinity calculation, lines 224–270"), which is not
   314	among my inputs and which I cannot audit. Per FALLACY-v2 (carrier/attainment; no filling
   315	a gap by analogy) I do not consume it.
   316	
   317	§3.2 supplies the missing low-band kill directly, in four lines, from ROW-NF alone.
   318	
   319	### 3.2 Third proof — the parametrisation forces `A` constant
   320	
   321	Assume `(3.1)`. Set `a(t) := A(r(t)²,q(t))`, `b(t) := B(r(t)²,q(t)) ∈ C[t]`. Since
   322	`F` vanishes on the image, `a³ = b²`.
   323	
   324	*Step 1: `a` has no root.* If `a(t_0)=0` then `b(t_0)²=0`, so the point
   325	`p_0 := (r(t_0)², q(t_0))` lies in `V(A,B) ∩ C²`. But `A(p_0)=B(p_0)=0` forces
   326	`F(p_0)=0` and, by Lemma 2.2, `p_0` is a singular point of `C` that is not an ordinary
   327	node. The affine singularities of `C` are exactly three ordinary nodes, so no such `p_0`
   328	exists. Hence `a = α ∈ C^*`.
   329	
   330	*Step 2: `A` is constant.* Substituting `x = r(t)²` (degree `6`, monic) and `y = q(t)`
   331	(degree `4`, monic), the six monomials of a degree-`≤2` polynomial acquire the **pairwise
   332	distinct** `t`-degrees
   333	
   334	```text
   335	   x² ↦ 12 ,   xy ↦ 10 ,   y² ↦ 8 ,   x ↦ 6 ,   y ↦ 4 ,   1 ↦ 0 .              (3.2)
   336	```
   337	
   338	Reading `a(t) = α` in descending degree gives, in order,
   339	`a_{20}=0`, then `a_{11}=0`, then `a_{02}=0`, then `a_{10}=0`, then `a_{01}=0`
   340	(each coefficient is the unique new contributor at its degree, the previous ones having
   341	already been set to zero). So `A ≡ α ∈ C^*`.
   342	
   343	*Step 3: contradiction.* Then `F = A³ − B² = α³ − B²`, of degree `6`, so `deg B = 3` and
   344	
   345	```text
   346	   F = (α^{3/2} − B)(α^{3/2} + B) ,
   347	```
   348	
   349	a product of two cubics — contradicting irreducibility of `F`. `[]`
   350	
   351	### 3.3 Fourth proof — projective Bézout against maximal contact
   352	
   353	Independent of §3.2, and degree-free in `A`:
   354	
   355	Assume `F̄ = G_2³ + G_3²`. As in §2, `C ∩ V(G_3) = V(G_2) ∩ V(G_3) ⊆ Σ(C)`, and by
   356	Lemma 2.2 no node qualifies, so
   357	
   358	```text
   359	   C ∩ V(G_3) = V(G_2) ∩ V(G_3) = { Q_∞ }     (nonempty by Bézout on the conic/cubic).
   360	```
   361	
   362	*`G_3` is smooth at `Q_∞`.* `mult_{Q_∞} F̄ = 2` (§1.3), while
   363	`mult(G_2³+G_3²) ≥ min(3·mult G_2, 2·mult G_3) ≥ min(3, 2·mult G_3)`. If
   364	`mult_{Q_∞} G_3 ≥ 2` the right side is `≥ 3`, forcing `mult F̄ ≥ 3`. So
   365	`mult_{Q_∞} G_3 = 1`.
   366	
