                                                                                Introduction
                                             The first systematic study on triple covers was done by Miranda [8]. Afterwards,
                                          triple covers are studied by many mathematicians (e.g. [2, 3, 14, 15]). Yet it is
                                          difficult to deal with general triple covers. For example, the following fundamental
                                          problem still remains as an open problem.
                                          Problem 0.1. Let ∆ = S + 2 T be a divisor on P2 = P2C , where S and T are re-
                                          duced divisors which have no common components. Give a necessary and sufficient
                                          condition for ∆ to be the branch divisor of a normal triple cover over P2 (see below
                                          for the notation).
                                             The above problem is an analogy to [5, Question 0.1]. The difference between
                                          Problem 0.1 and [5, Question 0.1] is whether a condition of ramification is given,
                                          or not. In some cases, Problem 0.1 was solved by some mathematicians, mainly
                                          Tokunaga, as follows:
                                             If S = 0, then a normal triple cover π : X → P2 with branch divisor ∆ must
                                          be a Galois cover, hence one can see an answer of Problem 0.1 from [8]. In the
                                          cases where (deg S, deg T ) = (2, 1), (2, 2), (4, 0) and (4, 1), Tokunaga solved Prob-
                                          lem 0.1 by using his theory of dihedral covers in [16] and [18]. Moreover, Yasumura
                                          showed that, if π : X → P2 is a normal triple cover with branch divisor ∆ and
                                          (deg S, deg T ) = (4, 1), then X is a cubic surface in P3 and π is a projection cen-
                                          tered at a point of P3 \ X ([20]). In the case where T = 0 and S is a sextic curve
                                          with at most simple singularities, Ishida and Tokunaga showed that X is either a
                                          quotient of an abelian surface by an involution or a normal cubic surface in P3 , and
                                          gave an answer of Problem 0.1 ([5]).
                                             The author is inspired by these results to do this study. The aim is to characterize
                                          normal triple covers over P2 with branch divisors of degree 6, and to give an answer
                                          of Problem 0.1 in the case deg ∆ = 6 without any assumptions.

